1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,505
Two notebooks from this lecture,
run by the instructors:

2
00:00:04,505 --> 00:00:06,381
the same data pipeline,

3
00:00:06,381 --> 00:00:10,539
the same 1,156 cheese photos,
the same five models.

4
00:00:10,859 --> 00:00:16,363
Notebook A crowns the fine-tuned MobileNet,
at 68.97 percent.

5
00:00:16,683 --> 00:00:20,473
Notebook B crowns the frozen MobileNet,

6
00:00:20,473 --> 00:00:26,304
also at 68.97 percent,
and puts the fine-tuned one at 62.93.

7
00:00:26,550 --> 00:00:29,729
So which recipe is better,
fine-tuning or freezing?

8
00:00:30,049 --> 00:00:33,569
And from these two tables, can you even tell
what was different?

9
00:00:33,889 --> 00:00:36,811
One clue is visible:
look at the epochs column.

10
00:00:37,131 --> 00:00:38,411
Hold that question.

11
00:00:38,731 --> 00:00:40,751
Part of the answer is in the table,

12
00:00:40,751 --> 00:00:43,809
and part of it is in what the table never
wrote down.

13
00:00:44,050 --> 00:00:46,781
Then the fine-tuned MobileNet leaves Colab.

14
00:00:47,101 --> 00:00:49,588
It gets packed into a 4 kilobyte tarball

15
00:00:49,588 --> 00:00:54,375
and trains on a machine you rent
for the length of one job, from one command.

16
00:00:54,695 --> 00:00:57,170
That command carries your W&B key.

17
00:00:57,417 --> 00:01:02,149
To follow along you need two
Colab copies with a free T4 GPU,

18
00:01:02,149 --> 00:01:07,657
a free account on W&B, Docker,
and a Google Cloud project with billing.

19
00:01:07,977 --> 00:01:13,993
Vertex AI jobs cost money while they run;
gold tags mark each point, without amounts.

20
00:01:14,313 --> 00:01:18,857
A yellow tag means pause and run it,
and the study guide has every command.

21
00:01:19,177 --> 00:01:23,678
Today's two words, from the lecture:
reproducibility and accountability.

22
00:01:24,717 --> 00:01:28,452
The lecture starts from three levers
for better deep learning:

23
00:01:28,452 --> 00:01:31,224
more data, better models,
and faster hardware.

24
00:01:31,544 --> 00:01:34,338
Today we pull the third one:
you rent hardware.

25
00:01:34,658 --> 00:01:37,368
And you add the bookkeeping that shows
whether it helped.

26
00:01:37,617 --> 00:01:39,252
On the Cheese App roadmap,

27
00:01:39,252 --> 00:01:43,718
the dashed box around experiment tracking
and baseline models is today.

28
00:01:44,038 --> 00:01:46,726
Your third milestone asks for exactly this.

29
00:01:46,967 --> 00:01:49,250
Where does each piece of an experiment go?

30
00:01:49,570 --> 00:01:54,017
Code to GitHub,
data to the versioning from lecture 11,

31
00:01:54,017 --> 00:01:58,060
and the environment
to pyproject.toml and uv.lock.

32
00:01:58,380 --> 00:02:01,047
Weights, metrics and logs have no home yet.

33
00:02:01,367 --> 00:02:04,138
A complete run should keep seven things:

34
00:02:04,138 --> 00:02:08,364
environment, code, data, model,
metrics, artifacts and notes.

35
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:13,976
The course uses Weights & Biases,
W&B for short, which is hosted for you.

36
00:02:14,296 --> 00:02:18,307
MLflow is the usual choice
if you must host it yourself.

37
00:02:18,550 --> 00:02:19,659
Here is the map.

38
00:02:19,979 --> 00:02:24,966
On top, the Colab lane:
notebook A fills a folder and uploads it,

39
00:02:24,966 --> 00:02:27,958
and notebook B streams its runs to W&B.

40
00:02:28,278 --> 00:02:32,257
Below, the repo lane:
your laptop packs the code,

41
00:02:32,257 --> 00:02:37,537
a bucket holds it, Vertex AI runs it,
and the run reports to W&B.

42
00:02:37,857 --> 00:02:39,265
Today we light both.

43
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:43,842
Open notebook A from the course page,
save a copy,

44
00:02:43,842 --> 00:02:47,596
and switch the runtime to a GPU;
pause and set it up.

45
00:02:47,916 --> 00:02:52,311
Its stored outputs show the instructors' run
on one Tesla T4.

46
00:02:53,667 --> 00:02:55,606
Run the dataset cells:

47
00:02:55,606 --> 00:03:01,070
1,156 photos from one GitHub release,
with four cheese labels.

48
00:03:01,390 --> 00:03:02,670
Remember their order.

49
00:03:02,990 --> 00:03:09,987
Then the split: 936 to train,
104 to validate, 116 to test.

50
00:03:10,307 --> 00:03:11,565
Pause and run them.

51
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:13,144
Something to ponder.

52
00:03:13,464 --> 00:03:22,253
The cell sets validation_percent to 0.2,
yet validation is 104 out of 1,156.

53
00:03:22,573 --> 00:03:24,323
What did the code actually do?

54
00:03:33,167 --> 00:03:35,130
Both splits use test_percent.

55
00:03:35,450 --> 00:03:39,840
10 percent of 1,156 is 116 for testing,

56
00:03:39,840 --> 00:03:47,268
then 10 percent of the remaining 1,040
is 104, the validation set.

57
00:03:47,588 --> 00:03:50,447
A small slip, copied into the repo trainer.

58
00:03:50,683 --> 00:03:52,240
Now the home-made tracker.

59
00:03:52,560 --> 00:03:56,592
Cell 31 makes a folder named
after the current Unix time.

60
00:03:56,912 --> 00:04:01,816
After each model trains, the helper
from cell 12 drops five files into it:

61
00:04:01,816 --> 00:04:06,256
the model, its weights, its structure,
its history and its metrics.

62
00:04:06,576 --> 00:04:08,582
Pause and train the five models.

63
00:04:09,367 --> 00:04:12,913
Cell 54 gathers the metric files
into one table,

64
00:04:12,913 --> 00:04:16,459
and cell 56 picks its top row
as the best model;

65
00:04:16,459 --> 00:04:19,266
pause and run through the predictions.

66
00:04:19,586 --> 00:04:23,713
A small slip:
accuracy becomes text before sorting,

67
00:04:23,713 --> 00:04:25,332
so it sorts strings;

68
00:04:25,332 --> 00:04:28,524
9.5 percent would rank above 68 percent.

69
00:04:28,767 --> 00:04:30,260
Here is a first answer.

70
00:04:30,580 --> 00:04:36,191
The folder keeps settings and scores,
but not which 116 photos were the test set.

71
00:04:36,511 --> 00:04:41,460
The split has no seed,
so every run is graded on a different 116.

72
00:04:41,780 --> 00:04:44,326
One photo is worth 0.86 points,

73
00:04:44,326 --> 00:04:48,351
and the winner
in notebook A leads by two photos.

74
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:55,640
Sign in with cell 62, put your own project
and bucket into cell 63, and run cell 66.

75
00:04:55,960 --> 00:04:57,549
Any bucket of yours works;

76
00:04:57,549 --> 00:04:58,848
if you have none yet,

77
00:04:58,848 --> 00:05:02,253
skip this upload and come back
after the setup chapter.

78
00:05:02,573 --> 00:05:03,725
Pause and run it.

79
00:05:04,045 --> 00:05:07,579
A gold tag:
those files now sit in your bucket,

80
00:05:07,579 --> 00:05:11,640
10 to 104 megabytes of weights per model,
plus a copy.

81
00:05:12,683 --> 00:05:14,752
Notebook B adds W&B.

82
00:05:15,072 --> 00:05:19,638
Its first cell installs it,
and the -U flag means whatever is newest.

83
00:05:19,958 --> 00:05:27,223
An extra check in our signed-out stand-in
shows 0.31.0 at the time of recording,

84
00:05:27,223 --> 00:05:30,219
while the stored run used 0.22.2.

85
00:05:30,539 --> 00:05:31,691
Pause and run it.

86
00:05:31,933 --> 00:05:35,933
The data cells, through cell 32,
match notebook A,

87
00:05:35,933 --> 00:05:41,213
but one also downloads a zip
of about 510 megabytes you never use.

88
00:05:41,533 --> 00:05:44,413
You may delete that line;
then pause and run them.

89
00:05:44,650 --> 00:05:48,703
Copy your API key
from the user settings on W&B.

90
00:05:49,023 --> 00:05:54,336
In Colab, open the key icon
and add a secret named exactly as on screen,

91
00:05:54,336 --> 00:05:56,106
with notebook access on.

92
00:05:56,426 --> 00:05:57,813
Pause and add yours.

93
00:05:58,133 --> 00:06:00,074
Cell 34 signs in with it.

94
00:06:00,316 --> 00:06:02,812
Now pause and run the five training cells.

95
00:06:03,132 --> 00:06:07,548
Each starts its run with wandb.init
in one project, cheese-app-demo.

96
00:06:07,868 --> 00:06:12,690
In the ledger, the five rows
become five runs on one shared epoch axis.

97
00:06:12,933 --> 00:06:15,216
But only the 2_block run shows curves.

98
00:06:15,536 --> 00:06:18,138
Compare its model.fit call
with another cell's.

99
00:06:18,458 --> 00:06:21,146
Something to ponder:
what do the other four lack?

100
00:06:27,983 --> 00:06:31,695
A callback:
code that model.fit calls after every epoch.

101
00:06:32,015 --> 00:06:35,604
Only the 2_block cell
passes the W&B callback,

102
00:06:35,604 --> 00:06:39,738
so only its loss and accuracy arrive,
epoch by epoch.

103
00:06:40,058 --> 00:06:43,099
Without one,
wandb.init gives you system charts,

104
00:06:43,099 --> 00:06:44,239
never your curves.

105
00:06:44,483 --> 00:06:48,920
Both MobileNet runs share one name,
so tell them apart by run ID.

106
00:06:49,240 --> 00:06:54,766
No cell finishes the last run,
so add wandb.finish after cell 52, an extra.

107
00:06:55,500 --> 00:06:59,105
Cell 55 builds the second table
from the opening.

108
00:06:59,425 --> 00:07:04,262
The fine-tuned MobileNet trained 10 epochs
here and 15 in notebook A:

109
00:07:04,262 --> 00:07:05,804
not a fair comparison.

110
00:07:06,124 --> 00:07:12,343
Now the frozen MobileNet,
with the same settings and epochs:

111
00:07:12,343 --> 00:07:15,660
67.24 in notebook A, 68.97 here.

112
00:07:15,980 --> 00:07:20,268
Two photos apart by chance alone:
the size of the winning margin.

113
00:07:20,516 --> 00:07:27,016
To upload, edit the bucket and project
inside cell 13 and run cell 13 again,

114
00:07:27,016 --> 00:07:30,607
then run cells 64 and 66; pause and do it.

115
00:07:35,150 --> 00:07:39,715
The lecture shows the pattern as three lines
around unchanged training code.

116
00:07:40,035 --> 00:07:41,977
Start the run with wandb.init.

117
00:07:42,297 --> 00:07:45,454
Watch the model
with a callback inside model.fit.

118
00:07:45,774 --> 00:07:47,715
And finish the run when training ends.

119
00:07:47,950 --> 00:07:49,806
Now notebook B's real cell.

120
00:07:50,126 --> 00:07:54,093
It starts the run,
then assigns a dictionary to wandb.config,

121
00:07:54,093 --> 00:07:56,825
renames the run, and calls wandb.run.save.

122
00:07:57,067 --> 00:07:58,411
Something to ponder.

123
00:07:58,731 --> 00:08:02,869
After that assignment,
and a later update with the execution time,

124
00:08:02,869 --> 00:08:04,875
what does the run's config hold?

125
00:08:13,717 --> 00:08:14,805
Watch the name.

126
00:08:15,125 --> 00:08:20,330
The call wandb.init points the name
wandb.config at the run's config drawer.

127
00:08:20,650 --> 00:08:23,105
The assignment does not fill that drawer;

128
00:08:23,105 --> 00:08:25,920
it moves the name onto a new,
loose dictionary.

129
00:08:26,240 --> 00:08:31,167
The update then writes into the loose one,
and the run finishes with its drawer empty.

130
00:08:31,417 --> 00:08:35,961
The proof, on today's W&B in offline mode,
as our stand-in.

131
00:08:36,281 --> 00:08:39,993
The name points at a plain dict,
and the run's config is empty.

132
00:08:40,313 --> 00:08:43,953
The fix passes the dictionary
to wandb.init itself,

133
00:08:43,953 --> 00:08:48,163
and the config holds all four keys
plus the execution time.

134
00:08:51,650 --> 00:08:56,068
The call wandb.run.save with the word
test uploads files matching test;

135
00:08:56,068 --> 00:08:57,623
it does not save the run.

136
00:08:57,867 --> 00:09:01,737
The repo's trainer gets the first
and last lines right:

137
00:09:01,737 --> 00:09:05,184
config inside wandb.init,
then update and finish.

138
00:09:05,504 --> 00:09:07,104
But the middle line is gone.

139
00:09:07,424 --> 00:09:11,291
One commit
in October 2025 removed the callback,

140
00:09:11,291 --> 00:09:12,822
without saying why.

141
00:09:13,142 --> 00:09:17,133
So its runs get config and system charts,
and no curves;

142
00:09:17,133 --> 00:09:19,627
exercise 1 adds the callback again.

143
00:09:20,667 --> 00:09:26,086
The second half of the lecture is Vertex AI,
Google Cloud's machine learning platform.

144
00:09:26,406 --> 00:09:30,021
Three console entries matter:
Workbench for notebooks,

145
00:09:30,021 --> 00:09:33,168
Training for jobs,
and Pipelines for workflows.

146
00:09:33,488 --> 00:09:38,672
Today is Training, on the custom jobs tab;
lecture 15 uses Pipelines.

147
00:09:38,917 --> 00:09:42,906
Serverless training means you rent
the hardware only for the training step.

148
00:09:43,226 --> 00:09:46,896
Nobody sets up a server,
and billing stops when the step ends.

149
00:09:47,216 --> 00:09:50,586
There is still a machine;
you just don't manage its lifetime.

150
00:09:50,834 --> 00:09:54,173
Step one:
move the notebook code into a Python file,

151
00:09:54,173 --> 00:09:56,935
and turn its hard-coded choices into flags.

152
00:09:57,255 --> 00:10:00,693
The flags
are epochs, batch size and model name,

153
00:10:00,693 --> 00:10:03,058
plus train_base and your W&B key.

154
00:10:03,300 --> 00:10:05,972
Step two packs that file into a tarball,

155
00:10:05,972 --> 00:10:09,380
one compressed archive,
and uploads it to a bucket.

156
00:10:09,700 --> 00:10:15,165
Step three runs from what the lecture
calls the Model Trainer CLI Container,

157
00:10:15,165 --> 00:10:19,193
our cockpit on your laptop:
it asks Vertex AI for a job.

158
00:10:19,513 --> 00:10:24,420
Vertex AI then starts a machine,
CPU only or with one T4.

159
00:10:24,740 --> 00:10:28,858
It loads Google's prebuilt TensorFlow image,
installs your tarball,

160
00:10:28,858 --> 00:10:32,484
runs your trainer with your flags,
and deletes the machine.

161
00:10:32,734 --> 00:10:35,017
Two facts drive everything after this.

162
00:10:35,337 --> 00:10:39,245
The job cannot see your laptop:
its data comes from the internet,

163
00:10:39,245 --> 00:10:42,974
and anything it should keep
must be written somewhere durable.

164
00:10:43,294 --> 00:10:45,704
And the image is Google's, not yours.

165
00:10:45,950 --> 00:10:47,230
A quick prediction.

166
00:10:47,550 --> 00:10:50,878
Your laptop has no GPU and no TensorFlow.

167
00:10:51,198 --> 00:10:54,782
Which machines in this picture
will ever import TensorFlow?

168
00:10:59,617 --> 00:11:04,353
Two: the Colab runtime,
and the container inside each Vertex job.

169
00:11:04,673 --> 00:11:08,449
Never the cockpit on your laptop;
the tarball chapter proves it.

170
00:11:09,483 --> 00:11:13,472
Clone the model-training repo
and move into it; pause and run it.

171
00:11:13,792 --> 00:11:16,907
Two extras show the course pin
and the repo's files.

172
00:11:24,316 --> 00:11:27,687
In the console, enable the Vertex AI API.

173
00:11:28,007 --> 00:11:31,484
Then request GPU quota,
an allowance per region.

174
00:11:31,804 --> 00:11:37,798
Filter for custom model training,
pick T4 GPUs, include us-central1,

175
00:11:37,798 --> 00:11:41,852
where cli.sh runs,
set a new limit and submit.

176
00:11:42,172 --> 00:11:43,452
Pause and do both.

177
00:11:43,772 --> 00:11:50,108
Approval may take hours or be refused;
cli.sh runs on CPU without it.

178
00:11:50,350 --> 00:11:55,512
Next, a secrets folder beside the repo,
never inside it; pause and make it.

179
00:11:58,866 --> 00:12:00,427
Then the identity:

180
00:12:00,427 --> 00:12:04,848
a service account model-trainer
with Storage Admin,

181
00:12:04,848 --> 00:12:08,780
AI Platform Admin
and Vertex AI Administrator,

182
00:12:08,780 --> 00:12:13,565
and one JSON key saved
in secrets as model-trainer.json.

183
00:12:13,885 --> 00:12:15,400
Pause and create yours.

184
00:12:19,300 --> 00:12:20,537
One honest note.

185
00:12:20,857 --> 00:12:24,763
This recording machine
has no Google credentials,

186
00:12:24,763 --> 00:12:27,791
no W&B account, no GPU and no browser.

187
00:12:28,111 --> 00:12:31,900
The key in that listing
is an obviously invalid stand-in,

188
00:12:31,900 --> 00:12:34,425
moved out before the container starts.

189
00:12:34,745 --> 00:12:38,470
So each cloud step shows
the failure a misplaced key gives,

190
00:12:38,470 --> 00:12:40,868
and successes come from named sources.

191
00:12:41,116 --> 00:12:44,529
Then create a bucket with your own name;
pause and create it.

192
00:12:44,849 --> 00:12:46,355
Two extras show why:

193
00:12:46,355 --> 00:12:49,894
our placeholder returns 404,
it exists nowhere,

194
00:12:49,894 --> 00:12:54,641
while the README's name returns 401,
it belongs to someone else.

195
00:12:54,961 --> 00:12:59,015
A gold tag: the bucket stores
your code and experiment folders.

196
00:12:59,250 --> 00:13:00,842
Last, copy your W&B key

197
00:13:00,842 --> 00:13:04,925
and export it in the terminal
you'll use for the container.

198
00:13:05,245 --> 00:13:06,418
Pause and do it.

199
00:13:06,738 --> 00:13:10,471
An extra echo prints its length,
40 for our placeholder.

200
00:13:10,791 --> 00:13:12,230
It must not be zero,

201
00:13:12,230 --> 00:13:15,826
and the trainer's pinned wandb
accepts exactly 40.

202
00:13:16,146 --> 00:13:18,800
If yours is a different length, keep going,

203
00:13:18,800 --> 00:13:23,058
but watch the chapter Who submits,
who runs, before you submit a job.

204
00:13:24,133 --> 00:13:25,477
Something to ponder.

205
00:13:25,797 --> 00:13:29,573
You exported the key, closed that terminal,
and opened a new one.

206
00:13:29,893 --> 00:13:31,792
What does the same echo print now?

207
00:13:38,633 --> 00:13:43,156
Zero: an exported variable exists only
in that shell and its children.

208
00:13:43,476 --> 00:13:46,932
Part 1 is done: the key is set,
the bucket has a name.

209
00:13:47,252 --> 00:13:50,366
Next, we build the cockpit,
and today it won't build.

